Stray separators in the header region of a SQL-mode data source. A banner
closed with `*/;` (the SSL-mode idiom) still lets the header split, so no
SSL diagnostic reaches the body (feature.diagnostics_pipeline A24), but
its `;` has no statement before it, and STARLIMS rejects the document, so
datasource_sql_semicolon fires on it. The doubled `;;` after :DSN is the
same case between directives; the scan continues past it, so the
:PARAMETERS line is still header and the body stays SQL (catalog
diag.datasource_sql_semicolon, issue #249). Directive terminators never
flag.
-- input.ds --
/* Data source: active couriers */;
:DSN := DATABASE;;
:PARAMETERS sZone := "N";

select c.courier_id, c.courier_name
from courier c
where c.zone_code = @sZone and c.active = 'Y'
-- format --
/* Data source: active couriers */;
:DSN := DATABASE;;
:PARAMETERS sZone := "N";

select c.courier_id, c.courier_name
from courier c
where c.zone_code = @sZone and c.active = 'Y'
-- diagnostics --
1:35-1:36 error datasource_sql_semicolon: Misplaced semicolon in a SQL data source. Nothing precedes it, so it is a stray statement separator - the body runs as a single SQL command and STARLIMS rejects the document with "Invalid SQL statement: remove any misplaced semicolons(;)". Delete it. (A ';' is required only after an SSL comment, and only in SSL documents.)
2:18-2:19 error datasource_sql_semicolon: Misplaced semicolon in a SQL data source. Nothing precedes it, so it is a stray statement separator - the body runs as a single SQL command and STARLIMS rejects the document with "Invalid SQL statement: remove any misplaced semicolons(;)". Delete it. (A ';' is required only after an SSL comment, and only in SSL documents.)
